  "preamble": "Loss of the glottal fricative [h] is among the most common consonant deletions. Word-initial h- disappeared from Latin already in the Classical period (hence its silence in all Romance languages), from Greek during the Koine period (psilosis), from most Mongolic languages after Proto-Mongolic, and from presyllables in several Chamic languages.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Elision, Consonant Loss",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Khalkha Mongolian",
      "languageFamily": "Mongolic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Mongolic *hulaxan 'red'",
          "to": "улаан ulaan /ˈʊɮaːŋ/",
          "note": "Initial *h- is lost in Khalkha and Buryat (ulaan) but kept in Dongxiang hulan."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Koine Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Attic ἥλιος /hɛ̌ː.li.os/ 'sun'",
          "to": "/ˈi.li.os/ (4th c. CE)",
          "note": "Psilosis: initial /h/ (rough breathing) was lost by the Koine period."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin homō, hominem 'man'",
          "to": "hombre /ˈombɾe/",
          "note": "Latin h was already lost in speech; Romance spelling h is silent."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
